## Introduction

**Welcome to the 3D Scanning Virtual Lab.**

3D scanning is the process of capturing the shape, size, and surface details of a real-world object or environment and converting it into a precise digital 3D model. Unlike traditional measurement methods that record only specific dimensions, a 3D scanner captures thousands to millions of surface points simultaneously, producing a complete digital representation called a point cloud.

The working principle of most 3D scanning technologies relies on light, time, or geometry. A scanner either projects light onto a surface and measures how it reflects back, calculates how long a pulse of laser light takes to return, or computes depth by comparing two camera images of the same scene. Each of these approaches produces a set of coordinates in three-dimensional space that together describe the shape of the scanned object.

3D scanning is the digital counterpart of physical measurement, where a traditional Vernier calliper measures one dimension at a time, a 3D scanner captures the entire geometry of a surface in a single session. This makes it especially powerful for complex shapes that cannot be fully described by simple measurements.

The applications of 3D scanning are widespread across engineering, medicine, architecture, and digital media. In manufacturing, it is used to inspect parts against their original CAD design. In healthcare, it is used to create custom prosthetics and implants. In archaeology and heritage preservation, it creates permanent digital records of irreplaceable artefacts. In robotics and autonomous vehicles, it provides real-time environment awareness through LiDAR sensors.

This virtual lab simulates the core principles and algorithms that power real 3D scanning systems. Each experiment isolates one part of the scanning pipeline — from acquiring depth data to processing, filtering, registering, and reconstructing a final 3D surface. By interacting with these simulations directly through sliders, toggles, and live visual outputs, students develop an intuitive understanding of concepts that would otherwise require expensive hardware and complex software environments.
